#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""FinePrint free Check CLI — one command, zero config for a first verdict.

The free tier is built for the most junior developer, or a person working
with a harness who is not a developer at all. The first run needs nothing:

  ./fineprint check

That one command finds your harness on its own, runs Catalogue v1
(the 16 built-in rules, FP-001..FP-016) against it, and writes a sealed
evidence bundle. No flags, no config file, no account, no network.

What it does, in plain words:
  - A "harness" is the coding agent you use (Codex, Claude Code,
    Muse Code, ...). A "policy" is the file of rules you wrote for it.
  - The check asks the harness's own policy checker what it decides for
    each rule: forbidden, prompt, allow, or unknown (it does not answer).
  - It never calls the harness company, never signs in, never uploads
    anything. Everything runs on your machine.

Exit codes (the whole contract — a script or CI job only needs these):
  0  pass — no rule was broken (unknowns are reported, not hidden)
  2  BLOCKED — at least one rule was broken, or a re-check regressed
  3  unknown/error — the check could not measure anything, or you asked
     for something that does not exist (bad harness name, missing file)

Commands:
  fineprint check [options]   run the check (no options needed, see above)
  fineprint init [--harness H] [--rules FILE] [--gate]
                              scaffold starter files (no flags = everything)
  fineprint verify BUNDLE     re-check a bundle's seal (0 = valid)
  fineprint report BUNDLE     print a bundle as a readable verdict table
  fineprint gate ...          the free wrapper guard (see `fineprint gate --help`)
  fineprint harnesses         list the harnesses this CLI knows

Power is behind flags, never in front of the first run: --harness,
--policy, --rules, --only, --out, --baseline, --bindings, --no-smoke.
See INTEGRATION.md for the full guide, and the README quickstart.
Stdlib only.
"""

